# A clause is a subject and its verb (`the tests pass`). A colon between
# two clauses packs two points into one sentence, so the fix ends the
# sentence at the colon or joins the clauses with `because` or `so`.
#
# A space in a Weir pattern matches a whitespace token, so the label
# exception sits directly against the colon. Harper splits `image:`
# into `image` and `:` with no whitespace between them.
#
# The two leading wildcards require two words before the word that
# carries the colon. Docs open lines with labels of every kind
# (`Usage:`, `Returns:`, `Step 1:`, `Known issue:`, a speaker name, a
# conventional-commit type). Each label is one or two words at the
# start of its sentence, so with the sentence scope no token precedes
# it and the pattern cannot start. A clause has a subject and a verb
# before its colon, so three words is the floor for a splice. The
# label list still covers a label that follows other words.
#
# After the colon, three signs mark a clause. One is an auxiliary, a
# helping verb such as `is`, `has`, or `can` (`the cache is cold`).
# Another is an adverb in the second or third position (`the tests
# always pass`), and the last is a verb in the third (`the first rule
# wins`). The pattern rejects a bare VERB
# tag in the first two positions, because the tagger marks participles
# (`-ing` and `-ed` verb forms) and many nouns as verbs. Without that
# limit, `the path passed by Claude Code`, `the value set by the
# environment`, and `the section on caching` all read as clauses. The
# pattern also rejects an adverb directly after a determiner such as
# `the` (`the only value`, `the always-on mode`). A pronoun such as
# `that` or `you` in the second position (`the tag that was pushed`,
# `the host you are targeting`) marks a noun phrase, a noun and the
# words attached to it, rather than a clause.
expr adverbs [never, always, also, only, silently, still, then, already, just, now, usually, often, sometimes, rarely, immediately, eventually]
expr aux [AUX, is, are, was, were, has, have, had, does, do, did, will, would, can, could, should, must, may, might, shall]
expr labels [Note, Warning, Tip, Caution, Important, Deprecated, TODO, FIXME, BUG, HACK, XXX]
expr rel [that, which, who, whose, where, when, it, you, we, they, I, he, she]
expr main * * !@labels: [([it, they, we, you, I, there, he, she] *), ([DET, PRON] [@aux, (* [@aux, @adverbs]), (* !@rel [@aux, @adverbs, <VERB, !PROG>])])]
let message "Colon between two clauses. End the sentence with a period, or join the clauses with `because` or `so`."
let description "Flags a colon that follows a clause and introduces a subject pronoun, or a determiner and a verb within three words."
let becomes ""
let kind "Punctuation"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "Note: the cache is cold on first run."
allows "Tip: you can skip the check."
allows "Run this command: make test"
allows "The options are: fast, slow, and medium."
allows "Default: the most recent tag."
allows "Deprecated: this method returns nil."
allows "TODO: it needs a lock."
allows "Usage: the program reads from stdin and writes to stdout."
allows "Returns: the value stored in the cache, or nil."
allows "Known issue: the cursor is hidden after exit."
allows "Step 1: you install the package."
allows "fix: the parser crashed on empty input."
allows "Alice: I think the cache is stale."
allows "Default: the only supported value."
allows "The hook has one input: the path that changed."
allows "The switch takes one argument: the host you are targeting."
allows "It ships two artifacts: a tarball built from source and a checksum."
